so the next topic in our program we  would like to highlight  has nothing to do with food but with  feelings  to be more precisely with empathy and i  would like to talk about this  with yasi hassan he is a social  psychologist for social change at the  hebrew university in jerusalem director  of research at arkor  hello shalom nice meeting you  hello nice to meet you together with his  colleague anat  amir who is a scientist and an artist  he has conducted a most interesting life  experiment  to try to increase empathy between  different groups and my first question  is can you  talk a little bit about empathy how do  you define  embassy actually  yeah it's a good question so empathy  involves sharing and understanding  the emotions and situation of others so  when a friend of mine  shares a sad story i would feel a bit of  his sadness  and if this friend is angry about  something i would feel part of this  anger as well so the ability to share  and understand  others is fundamental and important for  human interactions because  it leads us to care for each other  and how did your interest in this field  and this topic emerge what is  fascinating for you about this  yes so actually what is most fascinating  is the fact that although  empathy is important we find that people  tend to feel more empathy toward people  from their own group and less empathy  towards people  from other groups and this this  phenomenon called intergroup empathy  bias  and it becomes a barrier in promoting  intergroup  relation this is true almost in any  context  between ethnic groups religious group  national groups and political groups  and you can find this barrier in almost  any country  you can think about conservatives and  liberals in  in germany jews and arabs in israel or  christian and muslim in the united  states  and yes can you explain what is the  topic of  and the focus of your research and what  impact can it have on societies  yes so my research tried to understand  the mechanism actually what drives  people to feel empathy or to avoid  empathy in interview context and by  understanding this mechanism  i try to develop psychological  interventions to  overcome those barriers and to promote  empathy  between groups and the reason i'm doing  it is because i believe that  by finding way to increase empathy  between groups we can create a better  society so by increasing empathy would  be able to reduce negative attitudes  discrimination and violence between  groups and also try to  increase pro-social behavior and  solidarity between  green groups that's interesting  josie can we talk about the events you  are  offering at the berlin science summit um  the title of your session is believing  empathy  is unlimited um can you give us a  preview  into what happened during the experiment  that made you conclude this  yeah of course so in the last few years  anatomy and i have been developing  an innovative concept that combines live  performance art  and social psychology experiment that  aim to alter  to change people's basic assumptions  about the limitation of empathy  so in this collaboration between art and  psychology  it's been done through a framework of  theater performance that attract  audience 500  of hundreds that also act as  participants in empathy experiment and  they have the  the opportunity to interact with people  from their own group  and other groups and what we find is  that  when people tend to believe that empathy  is limited okay that they cannot  feel empathy toward many peoples the  truth  they keep their empathy to their own  group and they avoid  feeling empathy to toward other groups  but in our experiments and actually  performance  we we challenge jesus this belief and we  make them believe that empathy is  unlimited and what we find it when we  change this belief  people become more empathetic toward  others and even engage in more  pro-social behavior toward  others and this concept has been  tested and conducted in the united  states in israel  and we hopefully try to to do the same  in germany in half a year  and trying to increase empathy between  the general community and  and muslim community well thank you very  much  yossi hassan for this interview we're  looking forward to your session  it's a great way to learn more about  your own empathy  thank you so much
 

